{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "incident_category",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.95,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Categorizing incidents (network, VPN, database, printing) is a strong grouping axis for identifying operational burdens. Values align well with short_description text in all samples."
    },
    {
      "name": "failure_pattern",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Failure typology (connectivity, access_denied, service_down) gives a distinct analytical axis complementary to category. Samples are well grounded; 'access_denied' for 'unable to access database' is a reasonable reading."
    },
    {
      "name": "affected_resource_type",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.75,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Resource type helps target remediation. Mostly warranted, but some values are inferred beyond the text: 'server' for 'online database' and the dominance of 'server' (238) suggests confident specifics where descriptions are terse."
    },
    {
      "name": "incident_scope",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Scope (system_wide, user_specific) is a useful impact axis, but often not derivable from short one-line descriptions. E.g. 'Unable to access online database' labeled 'system_wide' is an unsupported inference; the text gives no scope signal."
    },
    {
      "name": "task_nature",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
      "source_groundedness": 0.7,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Break_fix vs access_request is a meaningful operational distinction. Mostly sound, but 'access_request' for 'Unable to access online database' is questionable—that reads as a break/fix access failure, not a request."
    },
    {
      "name": "cross_resolver",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.45,
      "source_groundedness": 0.3,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A cross-resolver flag could be useful, but it is not derivable from incident description text; it would require assignment/routing history. Values appear asserted without textual support and vary inconsistently across near-identical VPN rows."
    },
    {
      "name": "off_hours_incident",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.7,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Derivable from opened_at timestamps and useful for staffing/burden analysis. Sample values are consistent with the times shown (e.g. 18:14, 22:35 = off-hours true)."
    },
    {
      "name": "recurrence_signal",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.5,
      "source_groundedness": 0.35,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Recurrence would be relevant to operational burden, but a single incident's short_description rarely supports a recurrence claim; the values appear asserted without textual evidence (all samples 'false' with no grounding)."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
  "source_groundedness": 0.65,
  "rationale": "Most columns provide genuine, distinct grouping axes (category, failure_pattern, resource_type, scope, task_nature, off_hours) well-suited to analyzing operational burdens, and several are well grounded in the description text or timestamps. Weaknesses: cross_resolver and recurrence_signal assert properties not derivable from incident descriptions, and incident_scope/affected_resource_type sometimes over-specify beyond terse text. Overall useful but with notable groundedness gaps in the inferential flag columns."
}
